		<dict>
			<key>WFWorkflowActionIdentifier</key>
			<string>is.workflow.actions.comment</string>
			<key>WFWorkflowActionParameters</key>
			<dict>
				<key>WFCommentActionText</key>
				<string>Sync Health to Block 1

Reads yesterday's Apple Health numbers (Steps, Active Calories, Resting Heart Rate and Body Mass) and opens your workout-tracker web app with those numbers in the link, so the app can import them.

- Run it in the morning, so that yesterday is a complete finished day.
- You can add it as a personal Automation set to Time of Day 7:00 with Run Immediately turned on.
- Only the numbers that Health actually has for yesterday are added to the link. Missing numbers are left out completely.
- It runs silently: no prompts, no menus. It just opens the link in your browser.</string>
			</dict>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>WFWorkflowActionIdentifier</key>
			<string>is.workflow.actions.comment</string>
			<key>WFWorkflowActionParameters</key>
			<dict>
				<key>WFCommentActionText</key>
				<string>Shortcuts generated by Shortcuts Playground. May contain mistakes. Always check the shortcut's actions first.

This shortcut was created via the following user prompt:

&gt; An iPhone Shortcut called "Sync Health to Block 1" that sends yesterday's Apple Health numbers (steps, active energy, resting heart rate, body weight) to a personal workout-tracker web app by opening a URL with the values in the fragment, appending only the parameters that had a value.</string>
			</dict>
		</dict>
